1 NOTE Joseph of Arimathea, according to all four Gospels of the New Testament, a
2 CONT rich Jew of Arimathea, probably a member of the Sanhedrin, the ancient
2 CONT Jewish court in Jerusalem, who after the crucifixion of Jesus Christ,
2 CONT requested the body from the Roman procurator Pontius Pilate and placed it
2 CONT in his own tomb. According to some ancient writers he was later the
2 CONT founder of Christianity in Britain and of a monastery at Glastonbury;
2 CONT scholars, however, reject these claims. In the Arthurian cycle of romances
2 CONT and in late medieval legend he brings the Holy Grail into Britain.
