1 FAMS @F15@
1 FAMC @F16@
1 NOTE Mary (Virgin Mary), also the Virgin Mary, the mother of Jesus Christ,
2 CONT venerated by Christians since apostolic times. The Gospels give only a
2 CONT fragmentary account of Mary, mentioning her chiefly in connection with the
2 CONT beginning and the end of Jesus' life. Matthew speaks of Mary as Joseph's
2 CONT wife, who is "with child of the Holy Spirit" before they "came together"
2 CONT as husband and wife (see Matthew 1:18). After the birth of Jesus, she is
2 CONT present at the visit of the Magi (see Matthew 2:11), flees with Joseph in
2 CONT Egypt (see Matt. 2:14), and returns to Nazareth (see Matt. 2:23). Mark
2 CONT simply refers to Jesus as the son of Mary (see Mark 6:3). Luke's narrative
2 CONT of the nativity includes the angel Gabriel announcing to Mary the coming
2 CONT birth of Jesus (see Luke 1:27-38); her visit to her kinswoman Elizabeth,
2 CONT mother of John the Baptist, and Mary's hymn, the Magnificat (see Luke
2 CONT 1:39-56); and the shepherds' visit to the manger (see Luke 2:1-20). Luke
2 CONT also tells of Mary's perplexity at finding Jesus in the Temple questioning
2 CONT the teachers when he was 12 years old. The Gospel of John contains no
2 CONT infancy narrative, nor does it mention Mary's name; she is referred to as
2 CONT the mother of Jesus (see John 2, 19). She is present at the first of
2 CONT Jesus' miracles at the wedding feast of Cana (2:1,3,5) and at his death
2 CONT (19:25-27). Mary is also mentioned as being present in the upper room at
2 CONT Olivet with the apostles and with Jesus' brothers before Pentecost (see
2 CONT Acts 1:14).
2 CONT 
2 CONT The Early Church
2 CONT 
2 CONT As early as the 2d century Christians venerated Mary by calling her Mother
2 CONT of God, a title that primarily stresses the divinity of Jesus. During the
2 CONT 4th-century controversies concerning the divine and human natures of
2 CONT Jesus, the Greek title Theotokos (Mother of God) came to be used for Mary
2 CONT in devotional and theological writing. The Syrian monk Nestorius (died
2 CONT about 451) contested this usage, insisting that Mary was mother of Christ,
2 CONT not of God. The Council of Ephesus (431) condemned Nestorius's teaching
2 CONT and solemnly affirmed that Mary is to be called Theotokos, a title that
2 CONT has been used since that time in the Orthodox and Roman Catholic churches.
2 CONT 
2 CONT Closely allied with the title Mother of God is the title Virgin Mary,
2 CONT affirming the virginal conception of Jesus (see Luke 1:35). Initially,
2 CONT this title stressed the belief that God, not Joseph, was the true Father
2 CONT of Jesus. In the Marian devotion that developed in the East in the 4th
2 CONT century Mary was venerated not only in the conception, but also in the
2 CONT birth of Jesus. This conviction was expressed clearly in the 4th century
2 CONT baptismal creeds of Cyprus, Syria, Palestine, and Armenia (373-74). The
2 CONT title used was Aieiparthenos (ever-virgin), and by the middle of the 7th
2 CONT century the understanding of the title came to include the conviction that
2 CONT Mary remained a virgin for the whole of her life. The passages in the New
2 CONT Testament referring to the brothers of Jesus (for instance, Mark 6:3,
2 CONT which also mentions sisters; see 1 Corinthians 9:5; Galatians 1:19) have
2 CONT been accordingly explained as references to relatives of Jesus or to
2 CONT children of Joseph by a previous marriage, although no textual evidence
2 CONT supports these interpretations.
2 CONT 
2 CONT Beginning in the 2d and 3d centuries Mary was called Holy or Blessed
2 CONT Virgin to express the belief that, because of her intimate union with God
2 CONT through the Holy Spirit in the conception of Jesus (see Luke 1:35), Mary
2 CONT was completely free from any taint of sin. A Roman Council in 680 spoke of
2 CONT her as the blessed, immaculate ever-virgin.
2 CONT 
2 CONT In both the Eastern and Western churches, feast days in honor of the
2 CONT events of Mary's life came into existence between the 4th and 7th
2 CONT centuries. They celebrate her miraculous conception and her birth,
2 CONT narrated in the apocryphal protogospel of James (September 8), the
2 CONT Annunciation (March 25), her purification in the Temple (February 2), and
2 CONT her death (called the Dormition in the Eastern church) and bodily
2 CONT assumption into heaven (August 15; see Assumption of the Virgin).
2 CONT 
2 CONT The Middle Ages
2 CONT 
2 CONT During the late Middle Ages (13th-15th century) devotion to Mary grew
2 CONT vigorously. One of the principal reasons was the image of Christ that
2 CONT developed in the missionary efforts of the early Middle Ages. To the
2 CONT extent that the Gothic and other tribes of central and northern Europe
2 CONT were Christian, they remained strongly influenced by Arianism, a teaching
2 CONT that denied the divinity of Christ. In response, preaching and the arts of
2 CONT this period particularly stressed Christ's divinity, as in the Byzantine
2 CONT depictions of Christ as Pantokrator (universal and all-powerful ruler)
2 CONT and in the western images of Christ as the supreme and universal judge. As
2 CONT Christ became an awe-inspiring, judgmental figure, Mary came to be
2 CONT depicted as the one who interceded for sinners. As the fear of death and
2 CONT the Last Judgment intensified following the Black Plague in the 14th
2 CONT century, Mary was increasingly venerated in popular piety as mediator of
2 CONT the mercy of Christ. Her prayers and pleas were seen as the agency that
2 CONT tempered the stern justice of Christ. Among the popular devotions that
2 CONT came into being at this time were the rosary; (a chaplet originally
2 CONT consisting of 150 Hail Marys in imitation of the 150 Psalms in the
2 CONT psalter, later augmented by 15 interspersed Our Fathers as penance for
2 CONT daily sins); the angelus recited at sunrise, noon, and sunset; and
2 CONT litanies, invocations of Mary using such biblical titles as Mystical Rose,
2 CONT Tower of David, and Refuge of Sinners (see Litany). Hymns, psalms, and
2 CONT prayers were incorporated into the Little Office of the Blessed Virgin, in
2 CONT imitation of the longer divine office recited or chanted by monks and
2 CONT priests.
2 CONT 
2 CONT Doctrine of Immaculate Conception
2 CONT 
2 CONT The principal theological development concerning Mary in the Middle Ages
2 CONT was the doctrine of the Immaculate Conception. This doctrine, defended and
2 CONT preached by the Franciscan friars under the inspiration of the 13th
2 CONT century Scottish theologian John Duns Scotus, maintains that Mary was
2 CONT conceived without original sin. Dominican teachers and preachers
2 CONT vigorously opposed the doctrine, maintaining that it detracted from
2 CONT Christ's role as universal savior. Pope Sixtus IV, however, defended it,
2 CONT establishing (1477) a feast of the Immaculate Conception with a proper
2 CONT mass and office to be celebrated on December 8. This feast was extended to
2 CONT the whole Western church by Pope Clement XI in 1708. In 1854 Pope Pius IX
2 CONT issued a solemn decree defining the Immaculate Conception for all Roman
2 CONT Catholics, but the doctrine has not been accepted by Protestants or by the
2 CONT Orthodox churches. In 1950, Pope Pius XII solemnly defined as an article
2 CONT of faith for all Roman Catholics the doctrine of the bodily assumption of
2 CONT Mary into heaven.
2 CONT 
2 CONT Shrines
2 CONT 
2 CONT Marian shrines and places of pilgrimage are found throughout the world. At
2 CONT Monserrat in Spain the Black Virgin has been venerated since the 12th
2 CONT century. The icon of Our Lady of Cz粴ochowa has been venerated in Poland
2 CONT since the early 14th century. The picture of Our Lady of Guadalupe
2 CONT commemorates an alleged apparition of Mary to the Indian Juan Diego in
2 CONT Mexico in 1531. In the 19th century a number of apparitions of Mary were
2 CONT reported that inspired the development of shrines, devotions, and
2 CONT pilgrimagesfor instance, in Paris (1830, Our Lady of the Miraculous
2 CONT Medal), Lourdes (1858, Our Lady of Lourdes), Knock, in Ireland (1879, Our
2 CONT Lady of Knock), and Fatima, in Portugal (1917, Our Lady of Fatima).
